    "created_at": "2016-06-09T08:43:01Z",
    "updated_at": "2016-06-09T08:43:01Z",
    "body": "I agree that calling out individual features is sub-optimal. But we also can't say that \"attributes are ASCII case-insensitive\", they are not. See my comments above. In particular, an attribute that uses ASCII uppercase letters should never match, but they would if we said the match is ASCII case-insensitive.\n\nI think if we don't want HTML to define the behavior of individual CSS features, then CSS needs to do that instead in the way that is needed for HTML, and not just say \"depends on the host language\".\n\nSo HTML can say:\n\n> In HTML documents, HTML elements and namespace-less attributes of HTML elements are **compared against the lowercase version of a CSS feature**.\n\nThen CSS/Selectors can say something like:\n\n> The host language can define that certain elements or attributes are **compared against the lowercase version of a CSS feature**. This means that the following features must be converted to ASCII lowercase before comparing. The same feature when compared to other elements or attributes must be compared according to its original case. The case of the element or attribute name must not be changed and the comparison must be case-sensitive.\n> - Type selector\n> - The name part of an attribute selector\n> - The name part of the 'attr()' function\n\nAlthough attribute value selectors for some attributes are actually compared ASCII-case-insensitively, so that can't use the same thing as type selectors/attribute selectors...\n\n@fantasai @tabatkins other ideas on how we should define this?\n",
    "author_association": "MEMBER",
    "pin": null,
